            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of boiling and chlorination on the quality of stored drinking water and childhood diarrhoea in Indonesian households.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3294-3302</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1017/S0950268817002217</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We compared the impact of a commercial chlorination product (brand name Air RahMat) in stored drinking water to traditional boiling practices in Indonesia. We conducted a baseline survey of all households with children 1000 MPN/100 ml (RR 1·86, 95% CI 1·09-3·19) in stored water than in households without detectable E. coli. Although results suggested that Air RahMat water treatment was associated with lower E. coli contamination and diarrhoeal rates among children &lt;5 years than water treatment by boiling, Air RahMat use remained low.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
